A||N||3||Fleabitdog||wheel locks||||1||
Z||000000||Fleabitdog||04-27-2001||07:26 PM||||Does any one here know what size locknut socket is used on the Fiero? Last weekend skitime and I borrowed a set at a yard and it worked great. Today I was at another yard and borrowed thiers and the GM socket was to small. I would hate to buy a whole set for the one socket. ( besides thier set which was a smaller set did not have one that fit)Thanks  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||04-27-2001||10:03 PM||||if your talking about the 'key' socket to take off your locking nut, every one is supposed to be different, if you got another to work you were just lucky. My key will unlock lisas wheel, but hers wont fit mine. Mines just a hair smaller than hers.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fleabitdog||04-27-2001||10:11 PM||||No, there are sockets made for locking lugs that you don't have the key for. skitime and I just used a set last week. It is a set called wheelhubcap and locknut kit. The socket you hammer on the locknut, than use a 1/2" drive ratchet w/an extension. It works very well and does not damage the locknut.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-27-2001||10:40 PM||||There are several versions of those..... be glad a yard even bothered to buy one. usually they just reach for the blue wrench in most of the yards I've seen. ((They can't seem to grasp that a torch is a hell of allot more expesive to use than the unikey sockets....))||||reg||1
